# /qa-tester

Executable verify phase for UI work: take a test contract from the invoker (orchestrator, builder, or the user directly), drive a computer-use lane through every criterion, return one structured report. This skill tests and reports; it never fixes.

## When to use it

- A feature just shipped and its acceptance criteria involve a browser.
- The user describes expected behavior ("it should work like this") and wants it confirmed.
- Manual verification would cost the user many steps to do or figure out.

Not for anything a unit test or `verify.sh` can check — script that instead.

## Inputs (the test contract)

All three required before launch. Missing any → stop and get it; never improvise.

1. **Target** — localhost URL (from the worktree banner), staging, or production. If Cristian hasn't named the environment, ask him — never assume, and never default to production.
2. **Criteria** — acceptance criteria plus Cristian's own description of expected behavior, crystallized into per-criterion checks and passed verbatim. Do not reinterpret: a mismatch with the stated behavior is a flag even if the actual behavior seems reasonable.
3. **Setup** — login, test accounts, seed data.

**Jev pre-flight:** one batched call per `~/.claude/skills/jev/SKILL.md` — Nouls `criteria_browser_checkable`, `url_and_setup_present`. Either ≤0.5 → bounce the contract back to the invoker instead of burning a computer-use run.

## Project context file (optional)

A repo may carry standing QA context at `.claude/qa-tester.md` (one path, read by both harness twins). Check for it in Step 1: exists → load it before assembling the contract; missing → proceed on invoker-supplied context alone, silently. It holds what every run of that project needs: environment URLs, test-account pointers (never secrets — reference env files), a map of the system's flows, standing setup steps, known quirks not worth re-flagging. On conflict, the per-run contract wins — the file is background knowledge, not instructions for this run.

## Lane

- Cristian names a lane → use it; it overrides every rule below.
- Default in this harness: **Codex computer use**. The `~/.claude` twin defaults to Claude Chrome.
- Default lane low on usage → flip lanes; mark the report `quota override`.
- Both lanes equally constrained → ask Cristian which.
- Tester family == builder family → mark the report `same-family QA` so findings are weighed accordingly. Cross-family is preferred when free to choose.
- Routing hint (Cristian, 2026-09-22, unmeasured): long multi-step flows lean Codex computer use.

### Launch commands

Headless runs (`exec` / `-p`) have no one to answer permission prompts, so every tool call and site prompt is auto-denied. Headless needs the bypass flag; interactive runs send the prompts to Cristian.

```bash
# Codex computer use
codex exec --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox "<test contract>"   # headless
codex "<test contract>"                                                   # interactive

# Claude Chrome
claude -p --chrome --dangerously-skip-permissions "<test contract>"       # headless
claude --chrome "<test contract>"                                         # interactive
```

- Bypass flags remove approvals (Codex's also removes its sandbox). On production, paste the Production rule into the contract verbatim — the prompt is the only guard left.
- Codex still reports the site as denied → Cristian approves the domain once in the Codex Computer Use app's settings.

## Production rule

On production: observe-only — no record creation, no form submits with real side effects, no destructive clicks — unless Cristian explicitly authorizes that specific action. Local and staging: full interaction.

## Steps

1. Assemble the contract — first check the repo root for `.claude/qa-tester.md` and load it if present (Project context file above). Environment unstated → ask Cristian. Run the Jev pre-flight; bounce on failure.
2. Pick the lane per the rules above.
3. Drive every criterion to completion — at the app's pace, not the model's. After every navigation or click, wait for the page to settle (spinners gone, content rendered) before judging anything; verdicts on half-loaded pages are the main source of false flags. Before flagging a failure, reproduce it once more deliberately — wait, retry, then flag; a criterion that passes on the careful retry is `flaky`, not a failure. Slowness is an observation, not an error. A failure does not end the run: flag it, work around it, continue. Unreachable criterion → mark `blocked` with the reason. Lane fails to start (tool approval denied, site blocked) → switch to the other lane and mark the report `lane blocked: <reason>`; if Cristian named that lane, stop and report instead. Never report a lane that never ran as tested. Capture repro steps and screenshots for every failure as you go.
4. **Jev post-run triage** — one batched call at the end, never mid-run: per-criterion Choice over `{pass, fail, blocked, ask_user}`, severity Score per flag. Jev classifies what the lane observed; it never decides what to click and never sees screenshots.
5. Report once, to the invoker: per-criterion verdict; repro steps + screenshots per failure; improvement observations separated from errors; lane used plus any `quota override` / `lane blocked` / `same-family QA` marks; or a plain "all criteria passed". Findings go to the builder/orchestrator — never fix anything from this skill.

## `qa both` (named escalation only)

Run the identical contract blind in both lanes — no coordination between them. Agreements are high-confidence; disagreements are the product. Resolve factual splits against the app; bring judgment splits to Cristian, both sides steelmanned. Never the default — it doubles quota burn.

